## The three passes

```cpp
bool Cache::access(std::uint64_t addr) {
    const Decoded d = decode(addr);
    const std::size_t base = d.index * ways_;

    for (std::size_t w = 0; w < ways_; ++w)
        if (lines_[base + w].valid && lines_[base + w].tag == d.tag) {
            lines_[base + w].stamp = ++clock_;   // the line was USED
            ++hits_;
            return true;
        }
    ++misses_;

    std::size_t victim = ways_;
    for (std::size_t w = 0; w < ways_; ++w)
        if (!lines_[base + w].valid) { victim = w; break; }

    if (victim == ways_) {                        // every way is live
        victim = 0;
        for (std::size_t w = 1; w < ways_; ++w)
            if (lines_[base + w].stamp < lines_[base + victim].stamp) victim = w;
        ++evictions_;
    }
    ...
}
```

## Why the order matters

Searching for a victim before searching for a match would evict a line
that was about to hit. Counting an eviction before checking for an
invalid way would report evictions on a cold cache — the first `ways`
misses in a set fill it up and throw nothing out, and the test
`read 0 / read 100 / read 200 / read 300` on a four-way set expects
`0 evictions` for exactly that reason.

## LRU against FIFO, in five accesses

```
ways = 2        0    200    0    400    0
LRU            miss  miss  hit   miss  hit     (400 evicts 200)
FIFO           miss  miss  hit   miss  miss    (400 evicts 0)
```

Both policies agree on the first four lines. The fifth is the whole
difference, and it is why `stamp` has to be written on a **hit** and not
only on a fill. This is not a corner case invented for the test: a loop
over a working set slightly larger than the cache is the standard place
where the two policies come apart, and LRU is the one that keeps the
line you are about to use again.

## What associativity actually buys

Milestone 1 alternated between `0` and `0x40` and missed every time. Two
ways makes that pattern free. The cost is real — comparing `ways` tags
in parallel is `ways` comparators and a wider multiplexer — which is why
real L1 caches are 4- or 8-way and not fully associative.
